# XSH atomic_signal_fence(), atomic_thread_fence()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The two functions share one page (atomic_thread_fence.html is a copy).
# Fences are tested for what the synchronization rules oblige: data
# published before a release fence is visible after an acquire fence, and
# seq_cst fences forbid the store-buffering outcome.

assertion  XSH.atomic_signal_fence.001
anchor     functions/atomic_signal_fence.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atomic_signal_fence.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       atomic_thread_fence() can be called with each of the six memory
           orders and returns.
test       t/001-thread-fence-orders.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_signal_fence.002
anchor     functions/atomic_signal_fence.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atomic_signal_fence.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       atomic_signal_fence() can be called with each of the six memory
           orders and returns.
test       t/002-signal-fence-orders.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_signal_fence.003
anchor     functions/atomic_signal_fence.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A release fence followed by a relaxed atomic store of M
           synchronizes with a thread that reads that value of M with a
           relaxed load and then runs an acquire fence: ordinary data
           written before the release fence is visible after the acquire
           fence (20000 messages).
note       The wait for the last message is bounded (20 s); not seeing it is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/003-release-acquire-fences.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_signal_fence.004
anchor     functions/atomic_signal_fence.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A release fence followed by a relaxed atomic store synchronizes
           with a thread whose acquire load reads that value: data written
           before the fence is visible after the load (20000 messages).
note       Bounded wait as in XSH.atomic_signal_fence.003.
test       t/004-release-fence-acquire-load.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_signal_fence.005
anchor     functions/atomic_signal_fence.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A release store synchronizes with a thread that reads the stored
           value with a relaxed load and then runs an acquire fence: data
           written before the store is visible after the fence (20000
           messages).
note       Bounded wait as in XSH.atomic_signal_fence.003.
test       t/005-release-store-acquire-fence.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_signal_fence.006
anchor     functions/atomic_signal_fence.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       Two threads that each store 1 (relaxed) to their own object, run
           atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_seq_cst), and then load (relaxed)
           the other's object never both load 0 (20000 rounds, each started
           together).
note       The outcome is ruled out by the ISO C rule for a pair of seq_cst
           fences, which the page defers to; XBD 4.15.1.2 states only the
           single-fence forms (candidate standard issue).
test       t/006-seqcst-fences.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_signal_fence.007
anchor     functions/atomic_signal_fence.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A write to ordinary data, atomic_signal_fence(release) and a relaxed
           store, followed by raise() of a signal whose handler (same
           thread) reads the stored value relaxed, runs
           atomic_signal_fence(acquire) and reads the data, sees the data
           (2000 rounds).
test       t/007-signal-fence-handler.c
